Which, you know,
I don't necessarily appreciate it.

245
00:10:35,092 --> 00:10:36,093
-You don't subscribe?
-No.

246
00:10:36,177 --> 00:10:39,096
I feel you can be good at anything
that you get good at.

247
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:41,432
Do it long enough.
You don't have to not do something

248
00:10:41,515 --> 00:10:42,641
to be good at something else.

249
00:10:42,725 --> 00:10:43,601
Right.

250
00:10:44,977 --> 00:10:46,020
I mean, wrong.

251
00:10:46,103 --> 00:10:47,355
[laughter]

252
00:10:52,068 --> 00:10:53,694
Who said they're doing it
at the same time?

253
00:10:53,778 --> 00:10:55,279
They're not doing it at the same time.

254
00:10:55,363 --> 00:10:57,698
I'm saying there's only
a finite amount of time.

255
00:10:57,990 --> 00:10:59,867
You yourself don't do just one thing.

256
00:10:59,950 --> 00:11:01,827
I love it when people say, "You yourself."

257
00:11:01,911 --> 00:11:03,954
They really...
That's really going on the attack.

258
00:11:04,038 --> 00:11:05,998
-[both laugh]
-A gentle attack.

259
00:11:06,665 --> 00:11:08,167
-A cuddled attack.
-"You yourself."

260
00:11:08,250 --> 00:11:10,086
You yourself don't even do one thing.

261
00:11:10,169 --> 00:11:13,005
You won't like this
and/or agree with it...

262
00:11:13,089 --> 00:11:15,591
-[Mario] Mm-hmm.
-...but I would be better

263
00:11:16,008 --> 00:11:18,594
-at every... any one of these things...
-[Mario] Mm-hmm.

264
00:11:18,678 --> 00:11:20,513
...if I eliminated the other things.

265
00:11:20,596 --> 00:11:22,556
That's... You're just theorizing it.

266
00:11:22,640 --> 00:11:23,766
-How can you prove that?
-Yes!

267
00:11:23,849 --> 00:11:26,519
-You can't. It's not quantifiable.
-I don't know if I could--

268
00:11:26,602 --> 00:11:27,853
That's what I think.

269
00:11:27,937 --> 00:11:30,940
-We're having a coffee discussion here.
-It's very interesting.

270
00:11:31,023 --> 00:11:33,526
I say, when you focus, you're focusing.

271
00:11:33,859 --> 00:11:35,027
So there is no other thing.

272
00:11:35,111 --> 00:11:36,695
I'll try and agree with you on that.

273
00:11:36,779 --> 00:11:37,905
[laughter]

274
00:11:37,988 --> 00:11:39,865
Make an effort! [laughs]

275
00:11:40,574 --> 00:11:42,368
-"I'll try and agree." Good.
-Yeah.

276
00:11:44,120 --> 00:11:45,704
The reason, if I may...

277
00:11:45,788 --> 00:11:47,248
-It's your show.
-...you and I

278
00:11:47,331 --> 00:11:50,501
became glued to each other
from the day we met,

279
00:11:50,876 --> 00:11:53,003
was because we're both so...

280
00:11:53,629 --> 00:11:54,547
talkative.

281
00:11:54,630 --> 00:11:55,756
-Yes.
-[both laugh]

282
00:11:56,298 --> 00:11:58,300
I'm working with Jerry Seinfeld 
for the first time.

283
00:11:58,384 --> 00:12:00,219
-St. Louis, Missouri.
-St. Louis, Missouri.

284
00:12:00,302 --> 00:12:02,763
I actually asked the people
at the front desk what...

285
00:12:03,097 --> 00:12:04,849
what room you're in. [laughs]

286
00:12:04,932 --> 00:12:05,808
And they told me.

287
00:12:05,891 --> 00:12:07,351
[both laugh]

288
00:12:07,643 --> 00:12:09,603
So, I come to the door,
I knocked on the door,

289
00:12:09,687 --> 00:12:10,896
and I guess I look disheveled.

290
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:12,940
You opened the door
as long as the chain let it go.

291
00:12:13,023 --> 00:12:14,733
-Right. With the chain.
-Peaking out.

292
00:12:14,817 --> 00:12:17,027
"Hi, I'm Mario Joyner.
I'm your opening act."

293
00:12:17,111 --> 00:12:19,405
"Yeah? Okay. Nice to meet you."

294
00:12:20,030 --> 00:12:21,949
"I just wanted to say hi."
"Okay. Hi.

295
00:12:22,533 --> 00:12:24,076
I'll see you at the club." [door closes]

296
00:12:24,160 --> 00:12:25,119
He shuts the door.

297
00:12:25,202 --> 00:12:26,203
[both laugh]

298
00:12:26,287 --> 00:12:28,330
But then, at the club,

299
00:12:28,831 --> 00:12:29,957
I do my set.

300
00:12:30,040 --> 00:12:32,084
Said hi, talked about
some funny stuff that I said.

301
00:12:32,168 --> 00:12:34,003
-Yeah.
-This and that. We bonded right then.

302
00:12:34,086 --> 00:12:35,421
Although I annoyed you,

303
00:12:35,504 --> 00:12:37,381
-"I saw the act. He's funny."
-Right.

304
00:12:37,465 --> 00:12:39,592
-That was it.
-Yeah, the act is always the currency.

305
00:12:39,675 --> 00:12:41,802
-The act is the currency, yes.
-Yeah, yeah.

306
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:46,265
We've been doing the show for 35 years.

307
00:12:46,348 --> 00:12:48,893
Anyway, Jerry. I'm trying to tell you.
That's what I'm saying.

308
00:12:48,976 --> 00:12:52,188
-Yeah, that's what's interesting about it.
-We've been done done this show, come on.

309
00:12:52,271 --> 00:12:54,190
-Of course.
-Do that again. What is that thing?

310
00:12:54,273 --> 00:12:56,233
-We've been done done this show.
-No, no.

311
00:12:56,317 --> 00:12:58,694
-He should've had been done.
-He should've had been done that.

312
00:12:58,778 --> 00:13:00,988
Should've had been done that.
You invented a new tense.

313
00:13:01,071 --> 00:13:02,198
Yes. I... [laughs]

314
00:13:02,281 --> 00:13:03,616
Why is that a new tense?

315
00:13:03,699 --> 00:13:05,367
Because it's not past. It's not...

316
00:13:05,451 --> 00:13:07,953
Past would be "He should have done it."
That would be past tense.

317
00:13:08,037 --> 00:13:10,289
-He should've had been done that.
-"He should be doing it."

318
00:13:10,372 --> 00:13:13,000
That would be present tense. "Doing."

319
00:13:13,083 --> 00:13:15,920
I'm just trying to say
he's so late at doing it.

320
00:13:16,003 --> 00:13:19,215
Yes. "He should have been doing it
and doing it now," is what...

321
00:13:19,298 --> 00:13:21,675
You created a past future perfect.

322
00:13:23,427 --> 00:13:24,970
-Look how cute that looks.
-It's--

323
00:13:25,054 --> 00:13:26,388
-See that?
-"Cute". [laughs]

324
00:13:28,933 --> 00:13:29,975
I hate these turns.

325
00:13:32,019 --> 00:13:33,395
Why would they do that to you?

326
00:13:34,104 --> 00:13:37,441
-Why would you do that, Jim?
-Why'd she do that, Jim?

327
00:13:37,525 --> 00:13:39,610
-Who'd she call?
-Who'd she call?

328
00:13:39,693 --> 00:13:41,153
The State Attorney's office.

329
00:13:41,237 --> 00:13:42,655
The Attorney, um...

330
00:13:42,988 --> 00:13:43,948
Why...

331
00:13:44,031 --> 00:13:45,699
did she do that, Jim?

332
00:13:45,783 --> 00:13:47,451
Why'd she do that, Jim?

333
00:13:47,785 --> 00:13:49,245
-And he stops.
-Yes.

334
00:13:49,662 --> 00:13:50,829
-...Jim.
-[Jerry laughs]

335
00:13:54,333 --> 00:13:56,502
That's funny.
"Life can change in an instant."

336
00:13:56,585 --> 00:13:58,796
-What's that?
-There was a funny billboard there.

337
00:13:58,879 --> 00:14:01,590
"Life can change in an instant." 
It's a guy on a ladder on a roof,

338
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:02,883
and his hands have let go.

339
00:14:02,967 --> 00:14:06,053
Well, yeah. If you go up on a ladder
and let go,

340
00:14:06,136 --> 00:14:07,805
yeah, things are going to change.

341
00:14:07,888 --> 00:14:09,014
-Quickly.
-[Jerry laughs]

342
00:14:11,183 --> 00:14:14,311
-[Jerry] Look at the number of domiciles.
-[Mario] Look at 'em.

343
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:17,982
This is a renting, um, city.
People rent more here than anywhere.

344
00:14:18,065 --> 00:14:20,025
-They just rent, rent, rent. Yes.
-Really?

345
00:14:20,734 --> 00:14:23,070
I'm going from renting to renting.

346
00:14:23,237 --> 00:14:24,196
[laughs]

347
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:27,157
-You're going from renting to--
-I'm going from renting to renting.

348
00:14:27,241 --> 00:14:28,576
[Jerry laughs]

349
00:14:28,659 --> 00:14:30,160
Oh, so you wanna go from renting

350
00:14:30,244 --> 00:14:31,996
-to renting.
-Renting. Yes.

351
00:14:32,913 --> 00:14:34,665
The person that rents it
says they're renting,

352
00:14:34,748 --> 00:14:36,959
and the person that rents it out
says they're renting.

353
00:14:37,042 --> 00:14:38,752
-All renting.
-They use the same word.

354
00:14:38,836 --> 00:14:40,754
And they're doing the opposite thing.

355
00:14:40,838 --> 00:14:42,214
-They're both renting.
-Yes.

356
00:14:42,298 --> 00:14:44,383
One paying, one making.

357
00:14:44,717 --> 00:14:45,593
Takes two.

358
00:14:45,926 --> 00:14:48,721
-[Jerry laughs]
-You can't rent from no one.

359
00:14:49,054 --> 00:14:50,806
-I do it. I do it.
-The second rent--

360
00:14:50,890 --> 00:14:52,933
-The more pleased one that you do...
-Yes.

361
00:14:53,017 --> 00:14:55,686
-...is 'cause you're taking money in.
-Yes, of course.

362
00:14:55,769 --> 00:14:56,979
[both laugh]

363
00:14:57,062 --> 00:14:58,897
I'm going from renting to renting.

364
00:14:58,981 --> 00:14:59,815
[laughs]

365
00:15:03,027 --> 00:15:07,072
[Jerry] You're very handy,
and you're very good with, uh, home crafts

366
00:15:07,156 --> 00:15:08,782
and various types of, uh...

367
00:15:09,575 --> 00:15:12,703
Tools. I just learned about the, uh...
the hammer drill.

368
00:15:13,287 --> 00:15:15,873
-What's that?
-It's a drill... [laughs]

369
00:15:15,956 --> 00:15:17,124
It drills...

370
00:15:17,207 --> 00:15:19,376
It's the one you use
for drilling into concrete.

371
00:15:19,460 --> 00:15:22,087
You can drill into concrete
with a regular drill and the bit,

372
00:15:22,171 --> 00:15:24,006
but if you have a hammer drill,
what it does is,

373
00:15:24,089 --> 00:15:26,008
it hammers and drills at the same time.

374
00:15:26,091 --> 00:15:28,093
I say it reminds me of my younger years.

375
00:15:28,177 --> 00:15:29,553
[both laugh]

376
00:15:34,892 --> 00:15:37,102
Chicken and waffles and Pinkberry.

377
00:15:37,186 --> 00:15:38,437
We're back in the '90s.

378
00:15:39,939 --> 00:15:43,776
Nothing worse than a free man
not acting free.

379
00:15:43,859 --> 00:15:45,361
Goddamn right. It's a sad thing.

380
00:15:45,444 --> 00:15:47,446
What if I told you
you should have a motorcycle?

381
00:15:48,197 --> 00:15:49,448
It's fine to have kids.

382
00:15:49,531 --> 00:15:50,449
Have a motorcycle.

383
00:15:50,866 --> 00:15:53,327
-You don't have a kid, have a motorcycle.
-Mm-hmm.

384
00:15:54,370 --> 00:15:55,954
-Okay.
-Sometimes you say things

385
00:15:56,038 --> 00:15:57,831
just to see how it sounds
when it comes out.

386
00:15:57,915 --> 00:15:59,333
[laughing] Yes.

387
00:15:59,917 --> 00:16:01,460
What about downtown LA?

388
00:16:01,543 --> 00:16:02,628
-How about that?
-Nah.

389
00:16:02,711 --> 00:16:03,712
There's no beach!

390
00:16:03,796 --> 00:16:05,631
-I like the beach.
-You never go to the beach.

391
00:16:05,714 --> 00:16:07,967
"Mario, you never go.
You never get in the water." I don't.

392
00:16:08,050 --> 00:16:09,426
-No.
-But I wanna be near the beach.

393
00:16:09,510 --> 00:16:10,761
But you wanna know it's there.

394
00:16:11,095 --> 00:16:12,429
The homeless would agree with you.

395
00:16:12,513 --> 00:16:14,264
They get on that train,
they come down here.

396
00:16:14,348 --> 00:16:15,808
[Mario laughs]

397
00:16:15,891 --> 00:16:17,559
That train to Santa Monica from downtown.

398
00:16:17,643 --> 00:16:20,062
People who have no homes
are very particular about real estate.

399
00:16:20,145 --> 00:16:20,979
[Mario] Exactly.

400
00:16:22,731 --> 00:16:24,525
Wow, this is so nice.

401
00:16:24,733 --> 00:16:25,859
Nice, right?

402
00:16:27,528 --> 00:16:29,154
I can't get enough of this.

403
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:31,949
-Well, I can, and I think I have.
-[Jerry laughs]

404
00:16:44,086 --> 00:16:45,754
Yeah, Whitey Bulger, this neighborhood.

405
00:16:45,838 --> 00:16:47,297
He moved out here to head out,

406
00:16:47,381 --> 00:16:49,466
just changed his look and said,
"I'm staying here."

407
00:16:49,550 --> 00:16:51,552
Didn't get a big house,
got an apartment.

408
00:16:51,635 --> 00:16:52,845
That's thinkin'. That's smart.

409
00:16:52,928 --> 00:16:55,180
Didn't keep him out of jail,
but that's thinkin'.

410
00:16:55,264 --> 00:16:56,098
That's thinkin'.

